WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
In the 1950s, the world sprayed DDT to

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.960
wipe out malaria, and within years, many mosquitoes

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.080
could survive it.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.400
In 2014, Townsville tried something that sounds backwards.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.320
It released more mosquitoes, carrying a bacterium.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.440
After the bacterium spread through the city's mosquitoes,

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.200
local dengue stayed rare for about ten years.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.160
So how does controlling a vector cut infection,

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.560
and why did adding mosquitoes help?

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:32.886
A vector is a living carrier that takes

00:00:32.927 --> 00:00:34.886
a pathogen between hosts.

00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:37.606
Dengue is a virus carried mainly by the

00:00:37.687 --> 00:00:39.206
Aedes aegypti mosquito.

00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:42.246
A mosquito passes dengue on only if it

00:00:42.327 --> 00:00:45.286
bites an infected person, survives while the virus

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:48.326
multiplies, and then bites someone else.

00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:51.206
So there are three ways to cut infection:

00:00:51.287 --> 00:00:55.366
fewer mosquitoes, fewer bites, or mosquitoes that rarely

00:00:55.447 --> 00:00:56.646
pass the virus on.

00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:00.106
Start with the life cycle.

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:02.826
A female Aedes lays her eggs on the

00:01:02.907 --> 00:01:05.386
inside wall of a container, just above the

00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:07.946
water, in a bucket or an old tyre.

00:01:08.587 --> 00:01:11.386
When rain floods them, they hatch into larvae,

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:13.066
wrigglers that live in the water.

00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:16.026
Each larva becomes a pupa, and about a

00:01:16.107 --> 00:01:18.946
week after hatching, an adult flies off.

00:01:19.307 --> 00:01:22.186
Only females bite, because they need blood to

00:01:22.307 --> 00:01:23.066
make eggs.

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:27.466
So the simplest control is to take away

00:01:27.507 --> 00:01:28.346
the water.

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:31.386
Public health campaigns ask households to tip out

00:01:31.707 --> 00:01:35.866
containers, screen rainwater tanks and clear blocked gutters.

00:01:36.027 --> 00:01:38.506
Without water, the eggs don't hatch, so there

00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:41.186
are no larvae and no new adults.

00:01:41.547 --> 00:01:44.186
Changing places so a vector can't breed there

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:46.106
is environmental management.

00:01:46.187 --> 00:01:49.146
It's cheap and uses no chemicals, but it

00:01:49.227 --> 00:01:52.106
only works if nearly every household keeps doing

00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:52.426
it.

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:57.473
Where water can't be emptied, a pesticide, a

00:01:57.554 --> 00:02:00.673
chemical that kills pests, can kill the larvae,

00:02:00.754 --> 00:02:02.713
and adults can be sprayed too.

00:02:03.074 --> 00:02:07.313
Insecticides like DDT and the pyrethroids attack insect

00:02:07.474 --> 00:02:08.033
nerves.

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:10.833
And the vector's habits decide the method.

00:02:11.314 --> 00:02:14.993
Malaria's Anopheles mosquitoes mostly bite at night, so

00:02:15.074 --> 00:02:17.793
insecticide-treated bed nets protect sleepers.

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:21.713
Aedes bites by day, so in dengue outbreaks,

00:02:21.794 --> 00:02:24.833
Queensland teams spray the dark indoor corners where

00:02:24.994 --> 00:02:25.873
adults rest.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.780
But insecticides select resistance, like antibiotics.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.500
Mosquitoes that already carry resistance alleles survive the

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.020
spray and breed, so each generation holds more

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:39.500
of them.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.860
DDT resistance helped end the first attempt to

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.260
eradicate malaria, in 1969, and Anopheles resistance to

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:49.500
pyrethroids is now widespread.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.940
Sprays also kill other insects, and DDT builds

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.780
up along food chains, thinning the eggshells of

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:57.900
birds of prey.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.940
In the sterile insect technique, huge numbers of

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:07.260
males are reared, sterilised with radiation, and released.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.660
Wild females that mate with them lay eggs

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.180
that don't hatch.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.140
Male mosquitoes don't bite, and the released males

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.540
only affect their own species.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.340
But they leave no offspring, so releases must

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:22.060
continue, or the population bounces back.

00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:26.546
Genetic engineering does a similar job with a

00:03:26.627 --> 00:03:27.346
gene.

00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:30.466
Some male Aedes carry an inserted gene that

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:34.066
kills their female offspring before adulthood, so fewer

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:35.906
biting females emerge.

00:03:36.147 --> 00:03:38.906
Again, releases must keep going.

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:42.226
You've also met gene drives, genes that copy

00:03:42.307 --> 00:03:44.626
themselves into almost every offspring.

00:03:45.187 --> 00:03:47.426
A drive could spread through a wild population

00:03:47.507 --> 00:03:49.746
by itself, but it would be hard to

00:03:49.827 --> 00:03:52.386
reverse, and none has been released yet.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.720
Townsville's bacterium is Wolbachia.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.800
It isn't genetic engineering.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.400
No gene is inserted, and Wolbachia lives naturally

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:03.600
in many insects.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.960
Inside Aedes, it makes it much harder for

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.160
dengue to multiply, so the mosquito rarely passes

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.040
the virus on.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.720
And it spreads by itself.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.480
Infected females pass it to nearly all their

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.200
eggs, and their eggs hatch whichever male they

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:19.840
mate with.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.640
But when an uninfected female mates with an

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.360
infected male, her eggs don't hatch.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.200
So infected females out-breed the rest, and releases

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.000
can stop.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.040
So how do you judge these methods?

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.880
Ask how much they cut transmission, how long

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.600
the effect lasts, what they cost, and what

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.760
else they harm.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.000
Removing water is cheap and safe, but needs

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:44.960
everyone.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.800
Insecticides act fast, but select resistance and harm

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.040
other species.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.840
Sterile or engineered males are precise, but releases

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.280
have to keep going.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.400
Wolbachia keeps working after releases end.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.720
That's why programs combine methods.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:03.933
One line to keep.

00:05:04.494 --> 00:05:08.973
Cut vector-borne disease three ways: fewer mosquitoes, fewer

00:05:09.054 --> 00:05:12.653
bites, or mosquitoes that rarely pass it on.

00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:16.106
Two exam questions.

00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:17.346
First.

00:05:17.627 --> 00:05:20.586
A town sprayed insecticide X every year.

00:05:21.147 --> 00:05:23.786
Each year, mosquitoes were caught and exposed to

00:05:23.827 --> 00:05:24.906
a standard dose.

00:05:25.547 --> 00:05:28.506
Two per cent survived in 2016, nine per

00:05:28.627 --> 00:05:32.426
cent in 2018, thirty-eight per cent in 2020,

00:05:32.547 --> 00:05:35.786
and seventy-five per cent in 2022.

00:05:35.867 --> 00:05:38.746
Explain this trend, and propose one change to

00:05:38.787 --> 00:05:40.266
the town's mosquito control.

00:05:40.827 --> 00:05:42.186
Pause here to answer.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.320
Survival rose because spraying selected resistant mosquitoes.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.360
A few already carried resistance alleles.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:03.800
They survived, bred and passed the alleles on,

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:06.360
so each generation held a larger share.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.360
One change: remove breeding water, or switch to

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.400
a method insecticide resistance can't defeat, such as

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:14.200
Wolbachia.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.000
Saying the mosquitoes got used to the spray

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:18.760
is the classic mistake.

00:06:20.767 --> 00:06:21.726
Second.

00:06:21.887 --> 00:06:25.086
Town P releases sterile male Aedes every week

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:27.406
for two years, then stops.

00:06:27.727 --> 00:06:31.006
Town Q releases Aedes carrying Wolbachia for four

00:06:31.167 --> 00:06:32.966
months, then stops.

00:06:33.327 --> 00:06:36.046
Two years later, Aedes numbers in town P

00:06:36.127 --> 00:06:37.086
are back to normal.

00:06:37.647 --> 00:06:41.166
In town Q, most Aedes still carry Wolbachia,

00:06:41.247 --> 00:06:43.006
and local dengue stays rare.

00:06:43.647 --> 00:06:46.166
Explain the difference between the two towns.

00:06:46.447 --> 00:06:47.806
Pause here to answer.

00:06:59.034 --> 00:07:02.633
Sterile males leave no offspring, so when releases

00:07:02.714 --> 00:07:05.393
stopped, nothing kept the effect going, and the

00:07:05.434 --> 00:07:07.033
survivors bred back.

00:07:07.354 --> 00:07:10.393
Wolbachia passes from mothers to their eggs, and

00:07:10.474 --> 00:07:13.833
uninfected females mated by infected males lay eggs

00:07:13.914 --> 00:07:16.033
that don't hatch, so it stays in the

00:07:16.074 --> 00:07:17.353
population.

00:07:17.434 --> 00:07:20.473
And those mosquitoes rarely pass on dengue.

00:07:20.554 --> 00:07:23.593
Saying Wolbachia kills the mosquitoes is the classic

00:07:23.674 --> 00:07:24.313
mistake.

00:07:26.334 --> 00:07:29.053
So for any vector question, follow the life

00:07:29.134 --> 00:07:32.093
cycle, find the step a method attacks, and

00:07:32.174 --> 00:07:34.413
weigh how long it lasts against what it

00:07:34.534 --> 00:07:35.573
might harm.

00:07:36.014 --> 00:07:39.213
Next, what limits malaria and dengue control in

00:07:39.494 --> 00:07:40.013
practice.

00:07:42.034 --> 00:07:43.473
Here are the key notes.

00:07:43.714 --> 00:07:45.633
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:45.714 --> 00:07:46.513
to copy them.
